### Console & Diagnostics
- Use `read_console` to read Unity Editor Console logs — **essential for diagnosing compilation errors and runtime issues**.
  - `get_errors`: Get only errors (compiler errors + runtime errors) — use this FIRST when something goes wrong.
  - `get_warnings`: Get only warnings.
  - `get_all`: Get all log entries (errors, warnings, and info logs).
  - `get_count`: Get a summary count of errors/warnings/logs without full details.
  - `clear`: Clear the console.
  - `get_system_info`: Get Unity version, OS, processor, memory, graphics device, scripting backend, render pipeline.
  - `get_assembly_info`: List all loaded assemblies with name/version/location; supports `search` filter.
  - `get_scripting_defines`: Get all scripting define symbols for a build target group.
  - `set_scripting_define`: Add or remove a scripting define symbol (`enabled: true/false`).
  - `get_log_file`: Read the Unity Editor.log file; supports `max_lines` (tail) and `search` filter.
  - Use `search` parameter to filter entries by text (e.g., search for a specific file name or error code).
  - Use `max_entries` to control how many entries are returned (default: 50, max: 200).
- When a script modification causes compilation failure, ALWAYS call `read_console` (action: `get_errors`) to identify the exact error and file location.

### Scene Validation
- Use `validation` to validate scene and project quality:
  - `validate_scene`: Run all validators on the active scene — returns categorized issues (error/warning/info).
  - `validate_naming`: Check GameObject naming conventions (configurable pattern, default: PascalCase).
  - `validate_components`: Find missing scripts, missing references, and disabled components.
  - `validate_performance`: Detect performance issues (high poly meshes, too many lights, missing LOD, etc.).
  - `validate_layers_tags`: Verify all GameObjects use valid, defined layers and tags.
  - `validate_prefabs`: Find broken prefab instances and unapplied overrides.
  - `validate_audio`: Check AudioSource configurations (missing clips, 3D settings, etc.).
  - `validate_physics`: Detect physics issues (missing colliders on Rigidbodies, trigger mismatches, etc.).
  - `validate_ui`: Check UI hierarchy issues (missing EventSystem, overlapping elements, etc.).
  - `validate_custom`: Run a custom validation rule with a C# expression.
  - Use `severity_filter` to show only `error`, `warning`, or `info` level issues.
  - Use `fix_auto` to automatically fix simple issues where possible.

### Workflow Automation
- Use `workflow` for bulk GameObject operations and scene utilities:
  - `batch_rename`: Rename multiple GameObjects with a pattern supporting `{index}`, `{name}`, `{parent}`, `{index:00}` (zero-padded).
  - `batch_set_tag` / `batch_set_layer`: Set tag or layer on multiple GameObjects at once.
  - `batch_set_active` / `batch_set_static`: Toggle active state or static flags in bulk.
  - `find_replace_name`: Find-and-replace text in GameObject names (supports regex).
  - `collect_by_component`: Find all GameObjects with a specific component type.
  - `collect_by_tag` / `collect_by_layer`: Find all GameObjects with a specific tag or layer.
  - `snapshot_hierarchy`: Export the full scene hierarchy as a JSON tree (with component lists).
  - `batch_add_component` / `batch_remove_component`: Add or remove a component from multiple GameObjects.
  - `batch_move_to_parent`: Move multiple GameObjects under a new parent.
  - `count_objects`: Get statistics — total count, breakdown by tag/layer/component.
  - `list_scenes`: List all scenes (build settings + all .unity files + currently open scenes).
  - All modification actions support `dry_run: true` to preview changes without applying them.
  - All modification actions support `target_objects` (array of names/paths) or `target_tag`/`target_layer` for selection.

